--TEST--
The five relational operators agree on refusing every operand `rule:types/arithmetic` gives no ordering row for, and the object family keeps its own code — `rule:types/arithmetic`, `rule:classes/comparable`, `rule:enums/closed-integer-type`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Every spelling in `rule:types/arithmetic`'s `< <= > >= <=>` row, over the operand
// domains that row has no entry for. What is asserted is that the five
// *agree* — one code and one sentence per domain, whatever the operator —
// rather than that any one of them reports, and that the object family
// keeps its own code (`E0411`) however the receiver was spelled.
enum Rank {
    Bronze,
    Silver,
}

class Plain {}

string $s = "ab";
bytes $y = "ab" as bytes;
array<int> $xs = [1];
callable $f = fn (): int => 1;
Rank $r = Rank::Bronze;
object $o = new Plain();

// PHP orders every one of these by converting an operand first — two
// strings byte by byte, an array by length, `null` against `false`. ADR
// 0007 § 2 has no implicit conversion for any of them to be, so the
// ordering does not exist rather than answering something plausible.
mixed $a1 = $s < $s;
mixed $a2 = $s <= $s;
mixed $a3 = $s > $s;
mixed $a4 = $s >= $s;
mixed $a5 = $s <=> $s;

// One spelling each for the four remaining domains: the operator is not
// what decides, the operand is.
mixed $b1 = $y < $y;
mixed $c1 = $xs <=> $xs;
mixed $d1 = $f > $f;
mixed $e1 = null <= null;

// An enum case is a name, not its backing number (`rule:enums/closed-integer-type`), so it takes
// the same refusal with its own help.
mixed $r1 = $r < $r;

// The object family is `rule:classes/comparable`'s, and an erased receiver names no class
// to ask `Comparable` about — the same code the named-class refusal takes,
// so that "these two do not order" reads as one diagnostic.
mixed $g1 = $o < $o;

// Equality is untouched on every one of these domains: it is answered
// rather than ordered, and `rule:expressions/one-equality-operator` owns it.
echo ($s == $s) as string, ($xs == $xs) as string, (null == null) as string;
echo $a1, $a2, $a3, $a4, $a5, $b1, $c1, $d1, $e1, $r1, $g1;
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0715]: `<` has no meaning for `string`
   --> %s:25:13
   |
25 | mixed $a1 = $s < $s;
   |             ^^^^^^^ ordered here
   = help: `Core\Str::compare` is the ordering two strings have; `rule:types/arithmetic` tabulates no `<` for text, because PHP's own answer there is a conversion Novis never makes by itself

error[E0715]: `<=` has no meaning for `string`
   --> %s:26:13
   |
26 | mixed $a2 = $s <= $s;
   |             ^^^^^^^^ ordered here
   = help: `Core\Str::compare` is the ordering two strings have; `rule:types/arithmetic` tabulates no `<` for text, because PHP's own answer there is a conversion Novis never makes by itself

error[E0715]: `>` has no meaning for `string`
   --> %s:27:13
   |
27 | mixed $a3 = $s > $s;
   |             ^^^^^^^ ordered here
   = help: `Core\Str::compare` is the ordering two strings have; `rule:types/arithmetic` tabulates no `<` for text, because PHP's own answer there is a conversion Novis never makes by itself

error[E0715]: `>=` has no meaning for `string`
   --> %s:28:13
   |
28 | mixed $a4 = $s >= $s;
   |             ^^^^^^^^ ordered here
   = help: `Core\Str::compare` is the ordering two strings have; `rule:types/arithmetic` tabulates no `<` for text, because PHP's own answer there is a conversion Novis never makes by itself

error[E0715]: `<=>` has no meaning for `string`
   --> %s:29:13
   |
29 | mixed $a5 = $s <=> $s;
   |             ^^^^^^^^^ ordered here
   = help: `Core\Str::compare` is the ordering two strings have; `rule:types/arithmetic` tabulates no `<` for text, because PHP's own answer there is a conversion Novis never makes by itself

error[E0715]: `<` has no meaning for `bytes`
   --> %s:33:13
   |
33 | mixed $b1 = $y < $y;
   |             ^^^^^^^ ordered here
   = help: `rule:types/arithmetic`'s `< <= > >= <=>` row is the numeric types, plus two objects of one `Comparable` class (`rule:classes/comparable`); this operand is on neither half

error[E0715]: `<=>` has no meaning for `array<int>`
   --> %s:34:13
   |
34 | mixed $c1 = $xs <=> $xs;
   |             ^^^^^^^^^^^ ordered here
   = help: `rule:types/arithmetic`'s `< <= > >= <=>` row is the numeric types, plus two objects of one `Comparable` class (`rule:classes/comparable`); this operand is on neither half

error[E0715]: `>` has no meaning for `callable`
   --> %s:35:13
   |
35 | mixed $d1 = $f > $f;
   |             ^^^^^^^ ordered here
   = help: `rule:types/arithmetic`'s `< <= > >= <=>` row is the numeric types, plus two objects of one `Comparable` class (`rule:classes/comparable`); this operand is on neither half

error[E0715]: `<=` has no meaning for `null`
   --> %s:36:13
   |
36 | mixed $e1 = null <= null;
   |             ^^^^^^^^^^^^ ordered here
   = help: `rule:types/arithmetic`'s `< <= > >= <=>` row is the numeric types, plus two objects of one `Comparable` class (`rule:classes/comparable`); this operand is on neither half

error[E0715]: `<` has no meaning for `Rank`
   --> %s:40:13
   |
40 | mixed $r1 = $r < $r;
   |             ^^^^^^^ ordered here
   = help: an enum case is a name rather than a number (`rule:enums/closed-integer-type`); order the backing values instead — `($a as int) < ($b as int)`

error[E0411]: `object` names no class to check `Comparable` on; ordering two objects with `<`/`<=`/`>`/`>=`/`<=>` requires a class that implements it
   --> %s:45:13
   |
45 | mixed $g1 = $o < $o;
   |             ^^^^^^^ compared here
   = help: implement `Comparable`'s `compareTo(self $other): int` on the class

